# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1

# gsrpi-dev-base — the ONE base image for this fleet's dev containers (2d).
#
# This is dev/README.md § *Four copies of this* built: the ~700 lines that dd-dev,
# ds-dev, or3-dev and infra-dev agreed on, in one place. Debian trixie slim (glibc 2.41
# since 2026-09-21; bookworm and its 2.36 before that), Node + Claude Code (with the agent
# view off by baked managed settings), gh, zmx (what a slot lives in), Crush (on trial, the
# latest at build), Helix and the language servers it and Claude Code use, the everyday CLI
# tools (ripgrep, fd, jq, yq …), uv with ruff and ty, sshd, fish, the dev user, the entrypoint
# and every dotfile, and the two
# mountpoints claude-sessions arrives through from the hub. A repo's own dev/Dockerfile becomes a FROM line
# plus whatever that repo needs on top — see dev/Dockerfile here for the shape
# (ansible), or or3's for the other one (uv, and its own ssh identity).
#
# WHAT A CHILD IS EXPECTED TO CHANGE, and how. Four seams, none of which is a fork of a
# file in here:
#
#   environment    DEV_NAME, DEV_SECRETS_DIR, DEV_REPO, DEV_ENV_FILES —
#                  entrypoint.sh lists what each does. Set in infra's dev/compose.yaml,
#                  where every dev container's service is (2026-10-26).
#   ssh_config     COPY its own over /home/dev/ssh_config. The entrypoint reads that
#                  path, so a child's identities and hosts are its own business.
#   known_hosts.extra   COPY one in to pin a host key in ITS repo's tracked content
#                  (or3-dev pins or3ecr this way). Absent here, and absent is fine.
#   (sshd_config.d/ WAS a seam until 2026-10-27; sshd now reads only the base's
#                  sshd_config, and a drop-in a child still COPYs in is ignored.)
#   child-init.sh  COPY one in to /home/dev and the entrypoint RUNS it at start, after
#                  the pull and before sshd. The newest of them, added 2026-08-24: the
#                  four above all PLACE something, and dd-dev and ds-dev needed something
#                  DONE — `uv sync` against the lockfile in the mount, which cannot
#                  happen at build time because /workspace is not there yet. Non-fatal
#                  AND time-bounded — it runs ahead of the door, so a hang costs the
#                  door exactly as a crash would. Run with `bash`, so a child's own
#                  shebang is not honoured; entrypoint.sh has the reasoning.
#
# Anything a child cannot express through those five is a change to THIS file, made
# once, followed by a BASE_TAG bump. That is the trade the base image exists to make.
#
# REMOTE CONTROL LEFT THE FLEET on 2026-08-25, and with it a seam that used to be listed
# here: a child baked an rc-supervisor.sh, set DEV_REMOTE_CONTROL=1, and the
# entrypoint started it. Every container
# is now reached one way — ssh in, hold the work in a session (abduco until v0.4.0, zmx since) — so no child ships
# an rc-supervisor.sh and nothing here starts one. What runs in its place is the opposite
# job: `claude-sessions offload`, which STOPS a detached claude measured quiet for 10
# minutes, and leaves the conversation resumable — run in each container by the hub's timer
# over ssh since 2026-10-26, by this image's entrypoint before.
# The session holder is what makes a session survive a dropped link, and was therefore
# also what made an abandoned one hold a gigabyte of RAM for days. (Until 2026-10-06 that
# was offload-idle-claude.sh, with the new one as a dry run beside it — Stage A of
# plans/claude-sessions.md's Phase 5; Stage B swapped them and deleted the script.)
#
# Built and pushed to ghcr.io/gsfernandes81/gsrpi-dev-base (PUBLIC — pure toolchain,
# the same information as this file) by .github/workflows/dev-base.yml, **automatically,
# on any push to main that touches dev/** (2026-09-21; it used to want a manual
# dispatch). What that workflow actually enforces is the tag: it publishes a BASE_TAG
# that is new, does nothing for a push that changed no file this image is built from,
# and FAILS on a push that changed one without bumping BASE_TAG — because the tag below
# is a contract and main would otherwise describe an image that does not exist.
# Children FROM the ghcr name, so there is no cross-repo build order: no host needs this
# repo checked out to run a dev container. `make base` builds the identical image —
# but for Crush, which is that day's release (the Crush layer) — locally under the same
# name, as the offline fallback — and is also the way to have the
# image without waiting the ~5 minutes the workflow takes. infra owns
# the source because infra is where fleet-wide decisions live.
#
# THE TAG IS THE CONTRACT. bump BASE_TAG in dev/Makefile, rebuild, then move each
# child's ARG BASE_TAG when that repo is ready — children pin a tag, never `latest`,
# so a base rebuild cannot change a container behind its repo's back.
#
# ONE THING THE TAG DOES NOT PIN, since 2026-09-21: **Claude Code updates itself.** The
# tag still fixes every other byte in here, and a container still starts on the version
# the tag was built with — but that version is a floor, not a ceiling, and a container
# left running will move past it on its own. That is deliberate and the reasoning is at
# the node block below; it is stated here because this paragraph would otherwise read
# as a promise the image no longer keeps.
#
# The repo is NOT copied in: each container clones it into its own workspace volume at
# /workspace on first start (entrypoint.sh).
# Only the toolchain is baked. The entrypoint, sshd config and dotfiles here are
# infra-dev's, which were written to be the last copy: everything repo-specific in
# them is already an environment variable (dev/compose.yaml documents each).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS BEYOND TIDINESS, from dev/README.md: layer-sharing becomes
# guaranteed instead of a convention nobody can check; an entrypoint fix — already
# made three times — gets made once; and cloudflared's hash pin, which Cloudflare's
# deprecation clock makes a slow deadline, becomes one ARG here instead of four
# copies in four repos.

# ── abduco WAS BUILT FROM SOURCE HERE until 2026-10-06 ───────────────────────
# Trixie does not package it, so it was a compile stage. It went with Stage B: a slot is
# a zmx session since claude-sessions v0.4.0, and the one thing still listing abduco
# sessions, offload-idle-claude.sh, was deleted in the same change. dev/README.md has
# the history.

# ── the container itself ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TRIXIE, AND THE POINT OF IT IS THE LIBC. Debian 13 carries glibc 2.41; bookworm
# carried 2.36, and the floor this image is asked to clear is 2.39. Nothing else about
# the distro was the reason, which matters when reading what follows: everything below
# this line is unchanged from the bookworm image except where a comment says otherwise.
#
# WHAT CAME WITH IT, because a distro bump is never only the one package you wanted:
#
#   OpenSSH 9.2 -> 10.x   the door. sshd_config carried a claim MEASURED on 9.2, that
#                         `sshd -T` already reports `compression yes`; it is stated
#                         there now rather than inherited. (The other, about drop-ins,
#                         went with the drop-in seam on 2026-10-27.)
#   fish 3.6 -> 4.x       the login shell. config.fish uses `status is-login` and `cd`
#                         and nothing else, which is the least of fish's surface.
#   Python 3.13           unchanged — this is the same image line, so the entrypoint's
#                         python3 is the interpreter it always was.
#
# WHAT DID NOT CHANGE and might be expected to: the host kernels. glibc's floor here is
# far below Alpine's 6.x on zero and one, and the syscalls a newer glibc reaches for
# (clone3 and friends) are already in use by 2.36 on these same boxes under this same
# docker — so a seccomp refusal would have shown up long before this bump, not because
# of it.
#
# PINNED BY DIGEST since 2026.10.06.3, and the reason is the build cache, not
# reproducibility for its own sake. dev-base.yml reuses every layer whose step and inputs
# are unchanged, so a bump that only moves claude-sessions ships a few MiB instead of
# ~265 MiB to every Pi. A bare tag defeats that: Docker Hub rebuilds this one every few
# weeks, and the cache key of every layer below hangs off whatever the tag resolved to
# on build day. So a bump weeks after the last one would quietly be a full download
# again.
#
# MOVING THIS DIGEST IS THE REFRESH, and the only one for Debian and Claude Code. With the
# cache, the apt layer, NodeSource and the npm install of Claude Code never re-run on their
# own. (Crush is the one exception, and it is scoped to itself: its layer re-runs when
# Charm releases, ~25 MB, and refreshes nothing else — the Crush layer says how.) Debian
# security updates reach this image only when this line moves, and so do the fresher
# Claude Code (it updates itself anyway, so that part matters little) and apt's package
# set. Bump it on purpose, with a BASE_TAG bump, roughly monthly or on a Debian
# security notice: every Pi then pulls the whole image once. The digest is the
# MULTI-ARCH INDEX, not one platform's manifest, so it serves both legs of the build. Read
# the current one with `docker buildx imagetools inspect python:3.13-slim-trixie`, the
# `Digest:` line at the top.
FROM python:3.13-slim-trixie@sha256:bf44cdfcb76cd3b41e879bc058fc37ec5872002ccfde7fcb765e218cde0cd79c

ARG USERNAME=dev
ARG USER_UID=1000
ARG USER_GID=1000

# A SLOT IS A zmx SESSION (installed below, beside claude-sessions; abduco until
# 2026-10-06): a claude started outside one dies with the ssh session that carried it.
# screen stays because its scrollback and its status line are worth having for ordinary
# shell work.
# openssh-server is the in-container sshd, which here is simply the door — nothing
# tunnels back through it, unlike or3-dev's. openssh-client is what carries every
# ansible connection to zero, one and two, and is also what git needs for
# git@github.com (Debian's git does not pull an ssh client under
# --no-install-recommends). fish matches the shell on the phone and on zero,
# and is the dev user's login shell here — the /etc/shells line is added rather
# than assumed, because that is what `chsh` and anything else consulting it read.
#
# procps IS ALREADY HERE WITHOUT THIS LINE, and that is exactly why the line exists:
# openssh-server and fish both hard-depend on it, so `pgrep` and `ps` arrive as an
# auto-installed dependency of something else. The bring-up checks and anyone debugging
# need `pgrep`, and a tool present only because an unrelated package happens to pull it
# is the shape CLAUDE.md § *Landmines* records for iptables on `two`: it went missing days
# later, for a reason nobody would connect back. Naming it costs nothing and makes the
# dependency this image actually has visible in the file that declares them.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        git make screen fish openssh-client openssh-server procps \
        curl gnupg ca-certificates tzdata less iproute2 \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
    && if ! grep -qx /usr/bin/fish /etc/shells; then echo /usr/bin/fish >> /etc/shells; fi

# ── Node 22 LTS, and a Claude Code that can replace itself ──────────────────
# Claude Code is NOT pinned here and never was: `npm install -g` takes whatever the
# registry serves on build day. What changed on 2026-09-21 is that the copy it installs
# can now UPDATE ITSELF, which it could not before and did not say so where anybody
# would see it.
#
# WHY IT COULD NOT. `npm install -g` as root writes into /usr/lib/node_modules, which is
# root-owned; every session in this container runs as `dev`. Claude Code checks whether
# it can write its own install directory before it updates, finds it cannot, and
# mentions it once at start-up — inside a TUI, in a container people arrive at over ssh
# and then leave running for weeks. So the version baked on build day was the version
# you kept, and moving it meant a base rebuild plus a BASE_TAG bump in four repos, for a
# program that ships several releases a week. That is a pin nobody can keep up with, and
# what it actually produced was containers months behind with no signal saying so.
#
# WHAT MAKES IT WORK is one property and nothing else: the npm prefix is owned by the
# account that runs claude. /opt/npm-global, chowned to the dev user below, on PATH. The
# updater then rewrites /opt/npm-global/bin/claude in place, so every caller — an ssh
# session, `make claude`, the offloader matching on the process name — keeps naming the
# same thing and gets the new binary at its next start.
#
# NOT UNDER /home/dev, and that is the whole reason it is /opt rather than the
# conventional ~/.npm-global. Every dev container mounts named volumes over paths inside
# /home/dev — infra-dev has four — and a named volume only inherits the image's content
# while it is EMPTY. A binary baked under a volume's mountpoint is therefore visible on
# the first container and hidden by the first volume from then on, which is a broken
# `claude` on a rebuild months later for a reason nobody would connect back. Under /opt
# nothing can shadow it.
#
# `~/.local/bin` IS FIRST ON PATH for the one case this cannot control: that is where
# Claude Code's own installer puts its launcher, so if `claude install` is ever run in
# here, or an update migrates this to a native install, the newer of the two answers to
# `claude` rather than whichever was installed first.
#
# NODE AND NPM STAY IN THIS IMAGE FOR THIS, which is the trap the next tidy-up will
# meet: the `claude` npm installs is a NATIVE binary that does not run on Node, so
# nothing in ordinary use needs Node in here any more — but the updater for an npm
# install shells out to `npm`, and with npm off PATH it fails reporting "Check your
# internet connection", which points at the network rather than at the missing tool.
# Measured 2026-09-21 by running the update both ways round. Removing Node would look
# free and would silently stop this moving again.
#
# THE START STILL NEEDS NO NETWORK, which is the rule the baked collections and the
# baked cloudflared are here for as well. The image carries a working claude; the update
# is opportunistic, happens in the background of a session, and takes effect at the next
# start. A container with no route out runs what it was built with, exactly as before.
#
# TO TURN IT OFF: `DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER: "1"` in a child's compose `environment:`. That
# is Claude Code's own variable rather than a `DEV_*` of ours — a second name for a
# switch the vendor already has is a second thing to remember — and the entrypoint
# publishes the environment into ~/.ssh/environment, so it reaches ssh sessions too.
#
# ONE TRAP THIS CREATES: NPM_CONFIG_PREFIX is set for every user in the image, so a
# child that does `USER root` and `npm install -g` lands its package in this same tree,
# root-owned. That does not break claude — it already owns its own files — but it is how
# a root-owned file appears in a dev-owned directory. Install as `dev`, or chown after.
#
# NODESOURCE ON TRIXIE is the part of the libc bump most likely to be what broke, if
# anything did: the repository serves Debian 13 and Node 22, and the reports of trouble
# with it are apt's newer signature policy complaining rather than refusing. If a build
# ever fails in the line below rather than in the one after it, that is where to look —
# and the fallback is trixie's own `nodejs` (20.x), which costs an EBADENGINE warning
# from the claude-code package and nothing else, because that package's payload is a
# native binary that does not run on node.
ENV NPM_CONFIG_PREFIX=/opt/npm-global \
    PATH=/home/dev/.local/bin:/opt/npm-global/bin:/opt/claude-sessions/bin:$PATH
RUN curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_22.x | bash - \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends nodejs \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
    && npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code \
    && npm cache clean --force \
    && chown -R "${USER_UID}:${USER_GID}" /opt/npm-global

# GitHub CLI, from the release tarball rather than GitHub's apt repository — the
# same shape destiny-director's Dockerfile.dev uses, and one fewer signing key and
# sources.list entry in an image that already carries NodeSource's. GitHub's asset
# names use exactly the strings `dpkg --print-architecture` prints, so no arch
# remapping is needed. Bump GH_VERSION to upgrade; the pin matches dd's, so the two
# containers answer `gh --version` the same way.
#
# Not hash-pinned, unlike cloudflared and the binaries below, and the difference is
# deliberate: those run as what they download, this unpacks a signed release from the vendor's
# own host over TLS. What guards this one is the pin — an unpinned `latest` is a
# different binary on every rebuild with nothing to compare against.
ARG GH_VERSION=2.96.0
RUN set -eux; \
    arch="$(dpkg --print-architecture)"; \
    case "$arch" in \
      arm64|amd64) : ;; \
      *) echo "unsupported arch: $arch" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    curl -fsSL "https://github.com/cli/cli/releases/download/v${GH_VERSION}/gh_${GH_VERSION}_linux_${arch}.tar.gz" \
      | tar -xz -C /usr/local/libexec --strip-components=2 "gh_${GH_VERSION}_linux_${arch}/bin/gh"; \
    /usr/local/libexec/gh --version

# THE REAL gh IS IN libexec, and /usr/local/bin/gh is a three-line wrapper that hands it the
# hub's token for this container's repo, read fresh on each call (dev/gh says why). Beside it,
# git's credential helper for the same token, so a push over https uses it too. Both do
# nothing when /run/github/token is empty — the hub's broker off — and gh is then its own.
COPY gh /usr/local/bin/gh
COPY git-credential-hub /usr/local/bin/git-credential-hub
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/gh /usr/local/bin/git-credential-hub && gh --version

# ── the tools a person or a claude reaches for, and the language servers ─────────
# Added 2026-10-27 (owner: helix in the base, so every child has it, and every general tool a
# claude could use, LSPs included). In the BASE so each child repo gets them by its FROM line
# and none has to repeat a list. Children still add what only their repo needs (build
# chains, database clients, their own uv).
#
# From trixie where trixie has it, at trixie's version — security updates arrive with the
# base's python digest moving, as for everything else in this layer. `fd` and `bat` are
# Debian's `fdfind` and `batcat`, linked to the names everyone types.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        ripgrep fd-find jq tree file unzip zip xz-utils rsync sqlite3 \
        shellcheck shfmt bat fzf git-delta htop lsof strace bind9-dnsutils \
        netcat-openbsd wget patch bc psmisc \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
    && ln -sf /usr/bin/fdfind /usr/local/bin/fd \
    && ln -sf /usr/bin/batcat /usr/local/bin/bat

# The editor: Helix, from its own release, which carries the compiled tree-sitter grammars
# (trixie's `hx` is 25.01 and older). Hash-pinned per arch like every binary below, because
# it runs as what it downloads. HELIX_RUNTIME is where the grammars, queries and themes are.
ARG HELIX_VERSION=25.07.1
ARG HELIX_SHA256_arm64=ce23fa8d395e633e3e54c052012f11965d91d8d5c2bfa659685f50430b4f8175
ARG HELIX_SHA256_amd64=3f08e63ecd388fff657ad39722f88bb03dcf326f1f2da2700d99e1dc40ab2e8b
RUN set -eux; \
    case "$(dpkg --print-architecture)" in \
      arm64) a=aarch64; sha="${HELIX_SHA256_arm64}" ;; \
      amd64) a=x86_64;  sha="${HELIX_SHA256_amd64}" ;; \
      *) echo "unsupported arch" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    curl -fsSLo /tmp/hx.tar.xz "https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/releases/download/${HELIX_VERSION}/helix-${HELIX_VERSION}-${a}-linux.tar.xz"; \
    echo "${sha}  /tmp/hx.tar.xz" | sha256sum -c -; \
    mkdir -p /opt/helix; tar -xJf /tmp/hx.tar.xz -C /opt/helix --strip-components=1; rm /tmp/hx.tar.xz; \
    ln -sf /opt/helix/hx /usr/local/bin/hx; \
    HELIX_RUNTIME=/opt/helix/runtime hx --version
ENV HELIX_RUNTIME=/opt/helix/runtime \
    EDITOR=hx \
    VISUAL=hx

# Single-binary tools and language servers with no trixie package, hash-pinned per arch:
# taplo (TOML) and yq (YAML at the command line, mikefarah's — the one
# whose syntax every compose and ansible snippet online uses, not trixie's jq wrapper).
ARG TAPLO_VERSION=0.10.0
ARG TAPLO_SHA256_arm64=033681d01eec8376c3fd38fa3703c79316f5e14bb013d859943b60a07bccdcc3
ARG TAPLO_SHA256_amd64=8fe196b894ccf9072f98d4e1013a180306e17d244830b03986ee5e8eabeb6156
ARG YQ_VERSION=v4.54.1
ARG YQ_SHA256_arm64=189088da0c6429ec5178dfaab1a114805f6cab0b61b165ab236efedf1d57a71b
ARG YQ_SHA256_amd64=8e34fc298390875de416e6a4afcb8cabeceb25d9aa8506c1a2f9353cf702ea5f
RUN set -eux; \
    case "$(dpkg --print-architecture)" in \
      arm64) t=aarch64; y=arm64; \
             ts="${TAPLO_SHA256_arm64}"; ys="${YQ_SHA256_arm64}" ;; \
      amd64) t=x86_64;  y=amd64; \
             ts="${TAPLO_SHA256_amd64}"; ys="${YQ_SHA256_amd64}" ;; \
      *) echo "unsupported arch" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    gh_rel=https://github.com; \
    curl -fsSLo /tmp/taplo.gz "$gh_rel/tamasfe/taplo/releases/download/${TAPLO_VERSION}/taplo-linux-$t.gz"; \
    echo "$ts  /tmp/taplo.gz" | sha256sum -c -; gunzip -c /tmp/taplo.gz > /usr/local/bin/taplo; rm /tmp/taplo.gz; \
    curl -fsSLo /usr/local/bin/yq "$gh_rel/mikefarah/yq/releases/download/${YQ_VERSION}/yq_linux_$y"; \
    echo "$ys  /usr/local/bin/yq" | sha256sum -c -; \
    chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/taplo /usr/local/bin/yq; \
    taplo --version; yq --version

# Python's: uv, and ruff and ty as uv tools — Helix's own default Python servers are `ty` and
# `ruff`, and they are what or3, dd and ds lint with. Version-pinned from PyPI and the vendor's
# image, like Claude Code from npm. uv goes to /bin, where the children's own
# `COPY --from=…uv` lands too, so a child's uv replaces this one instead of being shadowed by
# it. A project's venv comes first on PATH where a child puts it there, so its pinned ruff
# wins over these inside that repo.
COPY --from=ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv:0.13.0 /uv /uvx /bin/
ARG RUFF_VERSION=0.17.0
ARG TY_VERSION=0.0.87
RUN UV_TOOL_DIR=/opt/uv-tools UV_TOOL_BIN_DIR=/usr/local/bin UV_PYTHON_DOWNLOADS=0 \
        uv tool install "ruff==${RUFF_VERSION}" \
    && UV_TOOL_DIR=/opt/uv-tools UV_TOOL_BIN_DIR=/usr/local/bin UV_PYTHON_DOWNLOADS=0 \
        uv tool install "ty==${TY_VERSION}" \
    && rm -rf /root/.cache/uv \
    && ruff --version && ty --version

# The node language servers, version-pinned, into the same npm tree as Claude Code: Helix's
# defaults for TypeScript/JavaScript, Bash, YAML and compose, JSON/HTML/CSS,
# Dockerfiles and fish. TypeScript stays on 5.x: 7.x is the native compiler and ships no
# tsserver, which typescript-language-server drives. NOT ansible-language-server: it wants
# Node 24, and moving this image's Node major for one server is its own decision. NOT pyright:
# Claude Code's Python server here is ty (claude-plugins/ below), which is lighter, and is what
# the repos type-check with (owner, 2026-10-27).
RUN npm install -g \
        typescript@5.9.3 typescript-language-server@6.0.2 \
        bash-language-server@5.8.1 \
        yaml-language-server@1.25.0 \
        @microsoft/compose-language-service@1.0.0 \
        vscode-langservers-extracted@4.10.0 \
        dockerfile-language-server-nodejs@0.15.0 \
        fish-lsp@1.1.5 \
    && npm cache clean --force \
    && chown -R "${USER_UID}:${USER_GID}" /opt/npm-global \
    && typescript-language-server --version && yaml-language-server --version

# cloudflared WAS BAKED HERE until 2026-10-26, hash-pinned, and started by the entrypoint
# when DEV_TUNNEL_HOSTNAME was set. The dev containers have no tunnel of their own any more:
# the hub is the way in (`ssh dev`), its tunnel a cloudflared sidecar in dev/compose.yaml,
# pinned there by digest. An inert 37 MB binary on a deprecation clock was all that was
# left of it here, so it went too.

# ── Crush: a second agent harness, on trial (owner, 2026-10-11) ──────────────────
# charmbracelet/crush, reached the plain way: `ssh -t <c> in-workspace`, then `crush`. No
# slot, no hooks, no environment — the provider key is typed at its first start and kept
# in ~/.local/share/crush, which every child already mounts a volume over. It is NOT in a
# zmx session, so a dropped link ends it; the claude-sessions menu knows nothing about it.
# Checked at 0.98.1: a plain `crush` leaves no process behind when it exits, although the
# binary has a `crush server` mode — a release that starts detaching one by default would
# be a process the offloader cannot see, so that is worth a look on each new version.
#
# THE LATEST, NOT A PIN IN THIS FILE — the owner's call — and CRUSH_VERSION is what makes
# that true. Crush has no updater, and Docker caches a layer by its instruction, not by what
# the instruction fetched, so an unpinned install would carry the first version it ever
# installed, forever (the npm layer above does exactly that to Claude Code, which gets away
# with it only by replacing itself). So the version is RESOLVED OUTSIDE THE BUILD — by
# dev/crush-latest, which CI and `make base` both run — and passed in: the layer misses the
# cache when Charm releases and only then, a bump that moves nothing else here costs no
# Crush download, and both architecture legs install the same version. What a tag carries
# is the release current when it was built; a forced rebuild or a local `make base` of the
# same tag later takes that later day's release. Placed above the dev user, claude-sessions
# and zmx so their bumps never rebuild this; a Crush release rebuilds them, all small.
#
# `CRUSH_VERSION=none` BUILDS WITHOUT IT — the escape for a day repo.charm.sh or GitHub is
# down and a base must still ship (`make base CRUSH_VERSION=none`). Unset, the build refuses
# rather than guessing. `crush --version` must then name exactly that version: it is a Go
# binary, which runs under the arm64 leg's QEMU, and it puts the version in CI's log.
#
# FROM CHARM'S SIGNED APT REPOSITORY, which is what lets it be unpinned here and still
# verified: apt checks the repository's signature, so the trust anchor is Charm's signing
# key rather than a per-version hash, and that key is what is pinned. ONLY THE PINNED KEY
# REACHES THE KEYRING: gpg.key is imported into a scratch home and the one certificate with
# that fingerprint exported — comparing the file's first fingerprint and keeping the whole
# file would also trust any key appended after it (found in review, reproduced). Not the npm
# package (@charmland/crush), a Node launcher whose postinstall fetches the binary unsigned;
# not a release tarball, whose checksums.txt vouches only for itself. The key expires
# 2027-07-13; an extension keeps the fingerprint, a new key fails here.
#
# apt READS ONLY charm.list, never the image's other sources: a Debian or NodeSource
# index that fails to fetch or verify must not fail a Crush layer, and nothing else here
# should be refreshed by it. The source and key are removed afterwards, so a child's own
# `apt-get update` never depends on repo.charm.sh either. The package, measured at 0.98.1:
# one static binary (86 MB on arm64, 25 MB as a .deb), no Depends, a man page and bash,
# fish and zsh completions.
ARG CRUSH_VERSION
ARG CHARM_APT_KEY_FPR=ED927B38BE981E53CA09153D03BBF595D4DFD35C
RUN set -eux; \
    case "${CRUSH_VERSION:-}" in \
      '') echo "CRUSH_VERSION is unset — pass --build-arg CRUSH_VERSION=\$(dev/crush-latest), or none (dev/Makefile's base target and dev-base.yml do)" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
      none) echo "CRUSH_VERSION=none: building without Crush"; exit 0 ;; \
      *[!0-9.]*) echo "CRUSH_VERSION=${CRUSH_VERSION} is not a version" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    GNUPGHOME="$(mktemp -d)"; \
    export GNUPGHOME; \
    curl -fsSLo /tmp/charm.asc https://repo.charm.sh/apt/gpg.key; \
    gpg --batch --quiet --import /tmp/charm.asc; \
    install -d -m 0755 /etc/apt/keyrings; \
    gpg --batch --export "${CHARM_APT_KEY_FPR}" > /etc/apt/keyrings/charm.gpg; \
    test "$(gpg --batch --show-keys --with-colons /etc/apt/keyrings/charm.gpg | awk -F: '$1 == "fpr" && !seen++ { print $10 }')" = "${CHARM_APT_KEY_FPR}" \
      || { echo "repo.charm.sh/apt/gpg.key does not hold the pinned key ${CHARM_APT_KEY_FPR}" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    echo 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/charm.gpg] https://repo.charm.sh/apt/ * *' > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/charm.list; \
    charm="-o Dir::Etc::SourceList=/etc/apt/sources.list.d/charm.list -o Dir::Etc::SourceParts=-"; \
    apt-get $charm update; \
    apt-get $charm install -y --no-install-recommends "crush=${CRUSH_VERSION}"; \
    rm -f /etc/apt/sources.list.d/charm.list /etc/apt/keyrings/charm.gpg /tmp/charm.asc; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* "$GNUPGHOME"; \
    test "$(crush --version)" = "crush version v${CRUSH_VERSION}"; \
    crush --version

# ── pi: a fourth agent harness, on trial, on its way to a slot (owner, 2026-10-30) ─────
# earendil-works/pi (pi.dev), reached like Crush and Jcode for now: `ssh -t <c> in-workspace`,
# then `pi`. Its model is qwen/qwen3.8-flash over OpenRouter, with the key read from the file
# the hub mounts at /run/github/openrouter-key and never from the environment (pi-models.json).
# The claude-sessions menu learns pi from a release of its own; until then pi has no slot and a
# dropped link ends it. plans/pi-harness.md is the design.
#
# VERSION-PINNED FROM npm INTO /opt/npm-global, beside the language servers, and bumped by a
# rebuild. PI_SKIP_VERSION_CHECK stops it asking pi.dev for a newer one at every start.
# --ignore-scripts costs nothing at 1.4.0: the only install scripts in its tree are a version
# warning in protobufjs and a no-op echo in @google/genai. The tree is chowned back to dev
# afterwards for the node block's reason: Claude Code updates itself there.
#
# STATE ON THE VOLUME, PACKAGES IN THE IMAGE. PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR puts pi's agent dir in
# ~/.local/share/pi, which every child mounts a volume over, so sessions survive a recreate. The
# packages cannot live there: a volume shows the image's content only while it is empty, so a
# package bump would never reach a container that had started once. `pi install` writes them
# to <agent dir>/npm and offers no other place, so the build installs them with the agent dir
# pointed at /opt/pi/seed, and the entrypoint links <agent dir>/npm to /opt/pi/seed/npm.
# /opt/pi/seed/LAYOUT names that directory for it (packages_dir=npm).
# PI_PACKAGE_DIR IS NOT THE SWITCH FOR THIS, although its name reads like one: it moves pi's OWN
# install (its package.json, themes and docs), and pointed anywhere else it leaves pi without
# them. Read from pi 1.4.0's config.js and package-manager.js.
#
# THE SEED is what the entrypoint copies into the agent dir: settings.json, into which each
# `pi install` below appends its package in install order, models.json, and the two extension
# configs. The package list is put back at every start and the rest only when absent; the
# entrypoint says which. tuiMode is regular because pi's default, fullscreen, takes the
# alternate screen and turns on mouse tracking, the pair that broke scrolling over ssh from the
# phone for Claude Code (claude-sessions issue #8). defaultProjectTrust is never, so pi skips a
# repo's .pi/ without asking: a trusted .pi/ could carry a gate config that replaces this one
# and packages that pi would install unpinned at start. The repos ship none, and the base's own
# packages and skills come from the agent dir, which project trust does not govern.
#
# THE PACKAGES, each pinned here; npm resolves their own dependencies once, in this layer:
#   pi-permission-classifier and @gotgenes/pi-permission-system, the judge and the gate. The
#     judge installs FIRST: pi runs session_start in package order, the gate announces itself
#     from its own session_start, and a judge listed after it misses that and joins only after
#     the first turn.
#     The gate's config turns the judge on by naming it in authorizerChain. Reads are allowed;
#     write and edit ask inside /workspace and are denied outside it; any path outside the
#     working tree (/workspace, where pi starts) is denied; bash and every other tool take the
#     gate's default, ask; and an ask nobody answers in five minutes is denied. The judge's
#     config is `{}`: the session model judges, and a failure defers to the gate's prompt and
#     never allows. The skills' real directory, /opt/claude-sessions/skills, is readable too.
#   cc-safety-net: fixed blocks on rm -rf, force-push, curl | sh and reads of secrets.
#   pi-subagents, pi-web-access and @nredd/pi-dev-tools.
#   pi-lens, diagnostics after edits. Its ast-grep binary arrives as an npm platform package
#     (@ast-grep/cli-linux-arm64-gnu or -x64-gnu) that @ast-grep/cli pins exactly, so npm's
#     registry integrity hash is the pin; the postinstall only links it into place. At run time
#     pi-lens would also fetch linters and language servers from GitHub releases into
#     ~/.pi-lens, unpinned and unhashed. PI_LENS_DISABLE_TOOL_INSTALL and
#     PI_LENS_DISABLE_LSP_INSTALL stop that, and it uses what this image already carries.
#   pi-powerline-footer, in plain characters (POWERLINE_NERD_FONTS=0 and the ascii preset)
#     because Termux's default font has no Nerd Font glyphs. Auto follow-up and working vibes
#     are off; both send what you type to a model outside the session's.
#   pi-themes, so /theme can match the terminal. The built-in dark stays the default.
#
# ROOT-OWNED, /opt/pi and all of it, unlike the npm tree: the gate's code and the seed it is
# restored from are what an agent in the session should not be able to edit. A package bump is
# a rebuild, as pi's own is.
ENV PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR=/home/dev/.local/share/pi \
    PI_SKIP_VERSION_CHECK=1 \
    PI_LENS_DISABLE_TOOL_INSTALL=1 \
    PI_LENS_DISABLE_LSP_INSTALL=1 \
    POWERLINE_NERD_FONTS=0
RUN npm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent@1.4.0 \
    && npm cache clean --force \
    && chown -R "${USER_UID}:${USER_GID}" /opt/npm-global \
    && if grep -rqF 'qwen/qwen3.8-flash' "${NPM_CONFIG_PREFIX}/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/bundle"; then \
         echo "pi's built-in OpenRouter list carries qwen/qwen3.8-flash; pi-models.json repeats it unchanged"; \
       else \
         echo "pi's built-in OpenRouter list lacks qwen/qwen3.8-flash; pi-models.json is what defines it"; \
       fi
COPY pi-settings.json /opt/pi/seed/settings.json
# A throwaway HOME, so npm's cache and logs land nowhere the image keeps. PI_OFFLINE keeps pi's
# own catalogue refresh out of a build; the installs are explicit and still run.
RUN set -eux; \
    export HOME=/tmp/pi-build-home PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR=/opt/pi/seed PI_OFFLINE=1; \
    for p in \
        npm:pi-permission-classifier@0.6.0 npm:@gotgenes/pi-permission-system@42.0.0 \
        npm:cc-safety-net@2.7.0 npm:pi-subagents@0.84.0 npm:pi-web-access@0.46.0 \
        npm:pi-lens@4.4.7 npm:@nredd/pi-dev-tools@0.7.0 \
        npm:pi-powerline-footer@0.22.0 npm:pi-themes@1.0.1; do \
      pi install "$p" || exit 1; \
    done; \
    test -n "$(ls -A /opt/pi/seed/npm/node_modules)"; \
    test -x /opt/pi/seed/npm/node_modules/@ast-grep/cli/ast-grep; \
    python3 -c 'import json; p = json.load(open("/opt/pi/seed/settings.json"))["packages"]; assert len(p) == 9 and p[0].startswith("npm:pi-permission-classifier@"), p'; \
    pi list; \
    rm -rf /tmp/pi-build-home
COPY pi-models.json /opt/pi/seed/models.json
COPY pi-permission-system.json /opt/pi/seed/extensions/pi-permission-system/config.json
COPY pi-permission-classifier.json /opt/pi/seed/extensions/pi-permission-classifier/config.json
# Parsed here, as the managed settings are, because each is hand-edited and none of them says
# much when it is malformed: the gate falls back to asking for everything, and the judge
# registers nothing.
RUN for f in /opt/pi/seed/settings.json /opt/pi/seed/models.json \
             /opt/pi/seed/extensions/pi-permission-system/config.json \
             /opt/pi/seed/extensions/pi-permission-classifier/config.json; do \
         python3 -c 'import json, sys; json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))' "$f" || exit 1; done \
    && printf 'packages_dir=npm\n' > /opt/pi/seed/LAYOUT \
    && chmod -R a+rX /opt/pi

# Create the dev user at the HOST owner's uid/gid, passed by the Makefile as build
# args (`make base`; the published base bakes 1001), so what it shares with the host — the
# secrets directories, the files a container writes into a mount — reads as gavin's. Guard against a
# uid/gid the base image already uses so the build cannot hard-fail on a collision.
# The login shell is fish, and that is the setting that makes it the default for
# `ssh infra-dev`: sshd runs the shell out of /etc/passwd and consults nothing else,
# so setting $SHELL or aliasing anything would not reach an ssh session. It is
# set with usermod as well as at useradd, so the collision branch — where the uid
# already exists and useradd is skipped — does not quietly keep /bin/sh.
RUN if ! getent group "${USER_GID}" >/dev/null; then \
        groupadd --gid "${USER_GID}" "${USERNAME}"; \
    fi \
    && if ! getent passwd "${USER_UID}" >/dev/null; then \
        useradd --uid "${USER_UID}" --gid "${USER_GID}" --shell /usr/bin/fish -m "${USERNAME}"; \
    fi \
    && usermod --shell /usr/bin/fish "$(getent passwd "${USER_UID}" | cut -d: -f1)" \
    && mkdir -p /workspace && chown "${USER_UID}:${USER_GID}" /workspace

# CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR moves ~/.claude.json INTO the .claude directory, so the single
# infra-claude volume carries both halves of the login and survives a rebuild.
# Without it, ~/.claude.json sits on the container filesystem and every rebuild
# re-runs onboarding on a container that has no one to answer it.
# claude-sessions (since v0.3.3) also derives a pre-0.3.3 slot's transcript path from it,
# closes a slot with no conversation rather than offloading it, and (v0.3.4) lists the
# menu's Closed group from $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/projects — so the offload loop, the menu
# and every claude must see the same value. Being an image ENV, they do; never set it
# for one and not the other.
# GH_CONFIG_DIR does the same for `gh auth login`: with no keyring in a slim image
# the token goes in hosts.yml, so that directory must sit on a volume.
# HISTFILE points bash history at the persisted volume for the same reason; fish's
# own history lands in ~/.local/share/fish, which is that same volume already.
# SCREENDIR: screen keeps its sockets in /run/screen, which a container image does
# not have and a non-root user cannot create, so an interactive `screen` over ssh
# would die with "Directory '/run/screen' must have mode 777". Deliberately NOT on
# a volume — a socket that outlived its container is a stale session `screen -r`
# would try, and fail, to attach to.
#
# ANSIBLE_CONFIG WAS HERE AND IS NOT ANY MORE — it moved to dev/Dockerfile, this
# repo's own child, on 2026-08-24. It names a path inside /workspace, and /workspace is
# a different repo in every container built on this base: in or3-dev it would point at
# a file that does not exist, and the entrypoint publishes the whole environment into
# ~/.ssh/environment, so it would have travelled into every ssh session there too. A
# setting that is only true for one child belongs in that child.
#
# CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AGENT_VIEW is the SECOND of the two switches that turn the agent
# view off. The authority is /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json, copied in below; this
# variable is here because the binary checks it FIRST, before any settings file is loaded,
# so it holds even in the states where the file does not — a malformed file is ignored
# whole, and a settings path that moves takes the file with it. The full reasoning is at
# that COPY.
#
# PATH AND NPM_CONFIG_PREFIX ARE NOT HERE. They are set at the node block above,
# because the build steps between there and here have to run with them — an ENV only
# applies from where it is written. What they are for is the whole of that block's
# comment: they are what lets Claude Code update itself.
ENV TZ=Etc/UTC \
    CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=/home/dev/.claude \
    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AGENT_VIEW=1 \
    GH_CONFIG_DIR=/home/dev/.config/gh \
    HISTFILE=/home/dev/.local/share/.bash_history \
    SCREENRC=/home/dev/.screenrc \
    SCREENDIR=/home/dev/.screen

# ── ROOT-OWNED to the `USER` line (in-workspace, managed settings, claude-sessions, the door);
#    everything after it belongs to dev ────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHERE THIS CONTAINER'S WORK IS, as a program. `in-workspace <cmd>` cds and execs, so a
# client that needs a command to run in the repo says `ssh <c> in-workspace …` and never
# names the path. (Until 2026-10-04 every login ran through it, as the first half of the
# client's `RemoteCommand in-workspace claude-sessions-door`; the door now cds itself.)
# It wraps the directory ONLY — not abduco, not a session name: the directory is the
# container's business, and holding a session across a dropped link is the client's.
#
# THIS MUST STAY ABOVE `USER`, and the first version did not. Placed among the COPYs at
# the bottom it looked right — they are all COPYs of files from this directory — but
# every one of those lands in dev's HOME and is dev-owned, while this is a program on
# PATH and must be root's. `chmod` then ran as dev against a root-owned file and the
# build died with "Operation not permitted". The boundary is the `USER` line, not the
# kind of instruction.
#
# COPY then RUN chmod, not `COPY --chmod=`: that flag needs BuildKit, and the `# syntax=`
# line at the top is a plain comment to the legacy builder rather than a requirement of
# it. Nor relying on git's executable bit, which is one `core.fileMode` away from being
# lost on a file that has to be executable or nothing runs it.
COPY in-workspace /usr/local/bin/in-workspace
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/in-workspace

# THE AGENT VIEW IS OFF FLEET-WIDE, AND THIS FILE IS THE AUTHORITY. Pressing ← at an
# empty prompt — the footer offers it, "← for agents" — starts a `claude daemon`, a warm
# `bg-spare` with its `bg-pty-host`, and further full sessions behind them. Measured
# 2026-10-01 against Claude Code 2.1.286, in a pty with a throwaway config: an idle
# session goes from ~250 MB RSS to ~1,260 MB on that one keypress, is still at ~1,250 MB
# a minute later at the prompt, and leaves ~690 MB running after the session itself
# exits. The leftovers were invisible to the old offload-idle-claude.sh, which walked down
# from abduco and found nothing above them. On a 4 GB Pi carrying four of these containers,
# that is the budget, spent by a keystroke nobody meant to press — or3-dev was holding
# ~700 MB of daemon and spares on 2026-10-01 for exactly this reason.
#
# MANAGED SETTINGS RATHER THAN ANY OTHER SETTINGS FILE, because this is fleet policy
# about zero's RAM and not a repo's preference. A project's .claude/settings.json and
# the user's own ~/.claude/settings.json both lose to this one, so no checkout and no
# login can turn the view back on. The cost is stated rather than discovered: `claude
# --bg` and `claude agents` do not work in these containers, the latter refusing by
# name ("disabled by the 'disableAgentView' setting"). That is the point, and
# plans/claude-sessions.md is the design that wants it.
#
# ROOT-OWNED, AND NOT UNDER /home/dev — both load-bearing. A policy file the account it
# governs can rewrite is not a policy; and every child mounts named volumes over paths
# inside /home/dev, where a baked file is visible on the container that created the
# volume and hidden from every one after it.
#
# THIS IS THE PATH THE INSTALLED BINARY READS, and that is checked rather than assumed —
# it was the one Phase 0 question left open, and it is now closed. Claude Code 2.1.286
# carries `default:return"/etc/claude-code"` for every platform that is not macOS or
# Windows, with a managed-settings.d drop-in directory beside the file; found by grepping
# the binary in this container on 2026-10-01, and the gate it feeds reads
#
#   if(a.CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AGENT_VIEW)return"is disabled by CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AGENT_VIEW";
#   if(aI()?.settings.disableAgentView===!0)return"is disabled by the 'disableAgentView' setting";
#
# which says three things worth keeping. The ENV VARIABLE IS CHECKED FIRST, before settings
# are loaded at all, so it is not a guess about where the file goes — it is the switch that
# survives the file being unreadable, ignored or moved by a later version. THE FILE TEST IS
# STRICT `=== true`, so the JSON boolean is load-bearing: `"true"` as a string does not
# disable anything. And the variable is tested for TRUTHINESS, so `=0` would disable it too
# — a thing to know before writing `=0` expecting it to re-enable the view.
#
# `make verify` prints BOTH switches and parses the file rather than grepping it, because a
# file in the wrong place, or one truncated after the word `true`, fails silently and looks
# exactly like one in force.
#
# SINCE claude-sessions v0.3.0 THIS IS ALSO WHAT KEEPS ITS ORPHAN SWEEP SAFE. A live
# `claude-sessions offload` kills any `daemon run --origin transient` tree whose parent is
# no longer a `claude` once it is ten minutes old — and agent view's supervisor outlives
# the session that started it ON PURPOSE, so with the view on, the sweep would kill working
# supervisors. Turning the view back on anywhere means stopping the offloader there first.
#
# THE SAME FILE PUTS CLAUDE CODE ON THE STABLE UPDATE CHANNEL (2026-10-07):
# `"autoUpdatesChannel": "stable"` — about a week behind `latest`, skipping releases with
# major regressions (code.claude.com/docs/en/setup). It is FORWARD-ONLY, and that is
# accepted (owner, 2026-10-07): the image carries whatever `latest` was when the npm layer
# above last ran — with the cache that is when the python digest last moved, not build day
# (`2026.10.07.1` carries 2.1.291) — and the updater never steps down, so a container stays
# on that version until stable passes it, then follows stable.
#
# A LONG-RUNNING claude REINSTALLS ITSELF EVERY 30 MINUTES AFTER AN UPDATE (measured
# 2026-10-08, claude-sessions#14). The updater replaces the file on disk, but the process
# keeps running the binary it started from, and the updater compares THAT version with the
# channel's: infra-dev's claude, started on 2.1.292, reinstalled 2.1.293 every half hour —
# ~250 MB each time, 13 GB a day of writes — until it exits. Stable makes the trigger
# rarer (it moves about weekly) but does not remove it; a restart of that claude ends it,
# and the offloader restarts idle ones anyway. or3-dev, on stable and already ahead of it,
# had nothing to install and did not loop. Checked in 2.1.292: the background updater and `claude update` both skip a
# channel version at or below the running one. Managed
# settings because it is one line in a file that already exists, and because nothing else
# baked survives: ~/.claude is a volume, and a default written into it would be hidden from
# every container after the one that created it. The cost, chosen: a container cannot be
# switched back to `latest` from inside it — that is an edit here and a base bump.
#
# AUTO MODE IS EVERY SESSION'S STARTING PERMISSION MODE (owner, 2026-10-29), as
# `permissions.defaultMode` in the same file, for the same reason: ~/.claude is a volume. It is
# a default, not a lock. Managed settings beat every settings file, so no repo's .claude/ can
# set another default, but Shift+Tab still changes mode inside a session and
# `claude --permission-mode default` starts one that asks (both checked on 2.1.311).
COPY claude-managed-settings.json /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json
# THE MANAGED POLICY — how an agent treats mail from another container (decisions.md § Agent
# mail). Claude Code loads /etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md as its *Managed* memory, which no repo's
# claudeMdExcludes can drop. The TEXT is not baked: it is infra's dev/hub/managed-CLAUDE.md,
# carried in the hub image and installed root-owned into the `policy` volume by the one-shot
# `policy` service in dev/compose.yaml, which every dev container mounts read-only at the
# directory made here. So an edit to the policy is a hub rebuild and `make up`, not a base
# bump. Root-owned all the way down, on purpose: the loader marks a managed file the session's
# own uid could have written (or with such a directory above it) as unverified and says so in
# the prompt — read from 2.1.311 and not seen to fire there, but the design costs nothing and
# the gate may turn on (docs/handoff/2026-10-28-agent-mail.md item 2). A dangling link, in a
# container run without the volume, reads as no file: proved in a sandbox, 2026-10-28.
RUN mkdir -p /etc/claude-code/policy && ln -s policy/CLAUDE.md /etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md
# CLAUDE CODE'S CODE INTELLIGENCE: a local plugin marketplace, read in place from /opt (no
# network, no auto-update), whose one plugin points Claude Code's LSP client at `ty server` for
# .py files, enabled for every container by the managed settings above. One server per
# extension is all Claude Code allows, so ruff stays the linter Claude runs, not a second .py
# server. The server starts the first time Claude edits a Python file, not with the session.
COPY claude-plugins /opt/claude-plugins
# What the plugin runs: `ty server` from the repo's own venv when it has a ty, else the base's
# (the file says in which order it looks).
COPY ty-lsp /usr/local/bin/ty-lsp
# Parsed here because it is hand-edited and Claude Code ignores a malformed settings file
# WHOLE and says nothing — the agent view would be back on, the channel back on latest and
# sessions back to asking for every tool call.
RUN chmod 0644 /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json \
    && chmod -R a+rX /opt/claude-plugins && chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/ty-lsp \
    && for f in /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json /opt/claude-plugins/.claude-plugin/marketplace.json \
                /opt/claude-plugins/ty-lsp/.claude-plugin/plugin.json /opt/claude-plugins/ty-lsp/.lsp.json; do \
         python3 -c 'import json, sys; json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))' "$f" || exit 1; done

# ── claude-sessions: NOT IN THIS IMAGE since 2026.10.26 — the hub installs it ───────
# The binary, the hooks drop-in that names it and the keep-alive skill arrive from the hub's
# `claude-sessions` volume, which dev/compose.yaml mounts read-only at /opt/claude-sessions in
# every dev container, and its managed-settings.d subpath over
# /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.d. So the version a container's hooks, agent and offload
# pass run is pinned once, in dev/hub/Dockerfile, and reaches every container at the hub's
# next start, with no base bump and no child bump (decisions.md, the hub rows).
# claude-sessions' docs/design.md § "The hub" is the design.
#
# What stays here is the two MOUNTPOINTS, root-owned and empty: /opt/claude-sessions, on
# PATH since the node block, and the managed-settings.d directory beside the agent-view file.
# A container started without the hub's volume — `docker run` by hand — therefore has no
# claude-sessions at all and no hooks: Claude Code reads an empty managed-settings.d as no
# drop-in, and the entrypoint says so. The hub's healthcheck is what keeps compose from ever
# starting one that way (depends_on: service_healthy).
#
# Until base 2026.10.26 the binary was pinned here (v0.4.7 last), with the drop-in generated
# from it at build time; git history has that block.
RUN mkdir -p /opt/claude-sessions /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.d \
    && chmod 0755 /opt/claude-sessions /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.d

# ── zmx: what a claude-sessions slot lives in (since claude-sessions v0.4.0) ──────
# Each slot is a zmx session: it holds claude across a dropped link, as abduco did, and
# unlike abduco it sends NO alternate-screen switch on attach — abduco's client did
# (`\e[?1049h`, every attach), and on the alternate screen Windows Terminal and Termux
# turn a wheel or a swipe into Up/Down keys, which claude reads as prompt history. That
# undid claude-sessions v0.3.8's own-scrollback fix; zmx is the owner's choice of
# replacement (2026-10-06, infra#9, claude-sessions' docs/design.md § "zmx, measured").
# zmx keeps a terminal emulator per session and replays its history on attach — about
# 1.08x the bytes, capped near 10,000 lines — which is the cost, measured and accepted.
#
# A PINNED RELEASE, HASH-CHECKED PER ARCHITECTURE, like claude-sessions above: static
# binaries from zmx's author, not in Debian. The hashes are of the TARBALLS, as
# claude-sessions' CI checks them; each holds one file, `zmx`.
#
# FETCHED FROM THE GITHUB RELEASE, NOT zmx.sh (2026-10-07). The author publishes the same
# tarballs both places — GitHub's asset digests are these hashes exactly — but zmx.sh
# began answering 404 for the aarch64 one from GitHub's runners while still serving it
# to zero, and its `s-maxage=604800` lets a shared cache hold an answer for a week, so a
# build could stay broken that long for nothing in this repo. The hash is what makes the
# source interchangeable; the release is GitHub's own copy, served by GitHub, not a mirror
# with a week-long cache in front of it.
#
# INSTALLED AS `zmx` AND NOTHING ELSE: a slot binds only when its claude's parent process
# is named `zmx`, and that name is the executable's file name — a copy under another name
# starts sessions that never bind (claude-sessions' own tests proved it by accident).
#
# ONLY EVER CHANGED BY A BASE BUMP: zmx's README says a version that changes its internal
# protocol ends running sessions, so an in-place upgrade is the wrong shape here; a
# recreate ends them anyway. With no ZMX_DIR set, as here, sockets land in /tmp/zmx-<uid>
# (gone with a recreate) and logs in ~/.local/state/zmx/logs (2 MB a session, then wiped);
# a ZMX_DIR moves both. No volume either needs.
#
# `zmx version` IS FATAL and compared exactly, the claude-sessions reasoning: a static
# binary has no excuse not to answer. Pointed at a throwaway ZMX_DIR because it creates
# its socket directory just to report it, and an empty /tmp/zmx-0 in the image would be a
# root-owned directory nobody asked for.
ARG ZMX_VERSION=0.8.1
ARG ZMX_SHA256_arm64=943eb44c812333fd450da12097521afd3339436e86f8c2ac618b905c4c9ece68
ARG ZMX_SHA256_amd64=dfd75720b942466f28870731cc86dbc07afa72fb8f3bd5eeb4ff707e4eecebe8
RUN set -eux; \
    arch="$(dpkg --print-architecture)"; \
    case "$arch" in \
      arm64) zarch=aarch64; sha="${ZMX_SHA256_arm64}" ;; \
      amd64) zarch=x86_64;  sha="${ZMX_SHA256_amd64}" ;; \
      *) echo "unsupported arch: $arch" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    curl -fsSLo /tmp/zmx.tar.gz "https://github.com/neurosnap/zmx/releases/download/v${ZMX_VERSION}/zmx-${ZMX_VERSION}-linux-${zarch}.tar.gz"; \
    echo "${sha}  /tmp/zmx.tar.gz" | sha256sum -c -; \
    tar -xzf /tmp/zmx.tar.gz -C /tmp zmx; \
    install -m 0755 /tmp/zmx /usr/local/bin/zmx; \
    rm -f /tmp/zmx /tmp/zmx.tar.gz; \
    test "$(ZMX_DIR=/tmp/zmx-build-check zmx version | awk '$1 == "zmx" { print $2 }')" = "${ZMX_VERSION}"; \
    rm -rf /tmp/zmx-build-check

# THE DOOR — what every ssh session runs, as sshd_config's ForceCommand (2026-10-04): a
# forwarded command as given, and otherwise a login shell in /workspace. Until 2026-10-26 it
# opened the claude-sessions menu at a terminal; the menu is the hub's now (dev/hub/door), and
# what reaches a container's door is the hub's own `ssh <c> claude-sessions agent` or a
# person's shell. Root-owned beside in-workspace for the same reason.
COPY dev-door /usr/local/bin/dev-door
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/dev-door

USER ${USERNAME}
WORKDIR /workspace

# Pre-create every directory compose mounts a named volume onto, AS the dev user.
# A volume that is empty on first use inherits the ownership of the directory it
# covers; created by root here, claude and ssh could not write to their own state.
# .screen is not a volume — it is here so the 700 screen insists on is set once,
# rather than left to the umask of whoever runs it first.
# .ssh/cp is ansible.cfg's ControlPath directory: ssh does NOT create it, and every
# task fails with "unix_listener: cannot bind" if it is missing.
#
# .local/bin is in the list for a different reason than the rest: nothing in this image
# puts anything there, and it is first on PATH. It is where Claude Code's own installer
# would land a launcher — so it exists, owned by `dev`, for an update that decides to
# use it rather than the npm prefix.
# EVERY VOLUME'S MOUNTPOINT IS MADE HERE, as `dev`: Docker gives a new named volume the
# owner and mode of the directory it mounts over, and a mountpoint the image lacks is made
# root's — `dev` then cannot write its own env volume (~/.env.d), which is how the smoke test
# found the same mistake in the hub on 2026-10-27.
RUN mkdir -p /home/dev/.claude /home/dev/.config/gh /home/dev/.config/fish \
        /home/dev/.ssh-host /home/dev/.ssh/cp /home/dev/.local/share /home/dev/.local/bin \
        /home/dev/.screen /home/dev/.env.d /home/dev/.local/share/pi \
    && chmod 700 /home/dev/.ssh-host /home/dev/.ssh /home/dev/.ssh/cp \
        /home/dev/.screen /home/dev/.env.d

# ── Jcode: a third agent harness, alongside Crush (owner, 2026-10-16) ─────────────────
# 1jehuang/jcode, a Rust CLI agent, reached the same plain way: `ssh -t <c> in-workspace`,
# then `jcode`. It is NOT offloaded and is not in a zmx session — like Crush, the
# claude-sessions menu and offloader know nothing about it. Unlike Crush that is a decision
# made on purpose: jcode is light enough not to need offloading, and its own 5-minute idle
# timeout reaps the background server it detaches (setsid()) when a session runs.
#
# PINNED AND HASH-PINNED, the cloudflared model, because jcode has no signed repository —
# only unsigned GitHub release tarballs with a self-vouching SHA256SUMS. A pin means a
# manual bump per release: JCODE_VERSION and the two per-arch hashes below. The tarball is
# one static ELF on arm64; on amd64 the `jcode-linux-x86_64` entry is a wrapper script that
# execs the real static binary, `jcode-linux-x86_64.bin`, which is what is installed.
#
# INSTALLED THE WAY JCODE ITSELF INSTALLS, so its own updater works. `jcode update` (and
# `--auto-update`, on by default) downloads a new release into ~/.jcode/builds/versions/
# and repoints the `stable` symlink; the launcher on PATH follows it. That only works when
# the launcher and ~/.jcode are writable by `dev` — a root-owned /usr/local/bin copy makes
# `jcode update` fail — which is why this layer runs as `dev` (it is past the USER line) and
# why the binary is not in /usr/local/bin. ~/.jcode is a volume (infra-jcode in compose.yaml),
# so an update and the provider login under config/jcode survive a recreate; the launcher at
# ~/.local/bin/jcode is in the image, first on PATH.
ARG JCODE_VERSION=0.98.1
ARG JCODE_SHA256_arm64=571917f4029b2f72c4045423871c40f30f93064f9938eb9f1910ea8f14797100
ARG JCODE_SHA256_amd64=449a8ed50aa9ac75e6afe9b8e916814172ff7cc63913c7ae1f9abd5b1bc5323c
RUN set -eux; \
    arch="$(dpkg --print-architecture)"; \
    case "$arch" in \
      arm64) sha="${JCODE_SHA256_arm64}"; jarch=aarch64; src=jcode-linux-aarch64 ;; \
      amd64) sha="${JCODE_SHA256_amd64}"; jarch=x86_64;   src=jcode-linux-x86_64.bin ;; \
      *) echo "unsupported arch: $arch" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    curl -fsSLo /tmp/jcode.tar.gz \
      "https://github.com/1jehuang/jcode/releases/download/v${JCODE_VERSION}/jcode-linux-${jarch}.tar.gz"; \
    echo "${sha}  /tmp/jcode.tar.gz" | sha256sum -c -; \
    tar -xzf /tmp/jcode.tar.gz -C /tmp "$src"; \
    mkdir -p "/home/dev/.jcode/builds/versions/${JCODE_VERSION}" \
             "/home/dev/.jcode/builds/stable"; \
    install -m 0755 "/tmp/$src" "/home/dev/.jcode/builds/versions/${JCODE_VERSION}/jcode"; \
    ln -sfn "../versions/${JCODE_VERSION}/jcode" "/home/dev/.jcode/builds/stable/jcode"; \
    printf '%s\n' "${JCODE_VERSION}" > "/home/dev/.jcode/builds/stable-version"; \
    ln -sfn "/home/dev/.jcode/builds/stable/jcode" "/home/dev/.local/bin/jcode"; \
    rm -f /tmp/jcode.tar.gz "/tmp/$src"; \
    case "$(jcode --version)" in \
      "jcode v${JCODE_VERSION}"*) : ;; \
      *) echo "jcode --version does not name ${JCODE_VERSION}" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    jcode --version

# THE BUILD'S OWN CHECK THAT CLAUDE CODE CAN STILL UPDATE ITSELF, run as `dev` because
# that is the account whose answer counts — it is the same question Claude Code asks
# before every background update, asked at build time where a wrong answer is a failed
# build rather than an image whose claude quietly never moves again. A missing chown, a
# child that re-roots the prefix, a future npm that lays the tree out differently: each
# one lands here.
#
# `claude --version` runs beside it and is DELIBERATELY NOT FATAL, which is the one
# place here that guesses rather than refuses. The arm64 leg of dev-base.yml builds under
# QEMU on a GitHub runner, this binary is a large self-contained one, and an emulator
# declining to run it would fail the whole base build for a reason that says nothing
# about the image — while the thing this file is actually asserting, the writability
# above, has already passed. A container that really did get a broken claude says so at
# its first start: the entrypoint prints the version in the boot log and `make verify`
# reads it off the running container.
#
# Pointed at a throwaway CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR: /home/dev/.claude is a volume mountpoint in
# every child, an empty volume inherits whatever the image left there, and config written
# by a build is not config anybody chose. HOME is named explicitly because a `USER` line
# does not set it for a RUN.
RUN test -w "${NPM_CONFIG_PREFIX}/lib/node_modules" \
    && test -w "${NPM_CONFIG_PREFIX}/bin" \
    && { HOME=/home/dev CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=/tmp/claude-build-check claude --version \
         || echo "WARNING: claude did not answer --version in this build (QEMU on the arm64 leg?) — the prefix is writable, so the build stands; check the container's boot log"; } \
    && rm -rf /tmp/claude-build-check \
    && { HOME=/home/dev PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR=/tmp/pi-build-check pi --version \
         || echo "WARNING: pi did not answer --version in this build; check the container's boot log"; } \
    && rm -rf /tmp/pi-build-check

# The in-container sshd: port 2222, non-root, key-only, no forwarding.
COPY --chown=${USER_UID}:${USER_GID} sshd_config /home/dev/sshd_config
EXPOSE 2222

# The baked half of ~/.ssh/config — github.com and the wildcard. The fleet blocks
# are prepended at start from the read-only secrets mount; see entrypoint.sh.
COPY --chown=${USER_UID}:${USER_GID} ssh_config /home/dev/ssh_config
COPY --chown=${USER_UID}:${USER_GID} screenrc /home/dev/.screenrc

# fish is the login shell, and this is the half of that which puts every shell in
# /workspace rather than in the home directory. Not on a volume, so a rebuild is
# what updates it.
COPY --chown=${USER_UID}:${USER_GID} config.fish /home/dev/.config/fish/config.fish

# The interactive login walkthrough, run by `make login` from a terminal on zero.
# It lives in the IMAGE rather than in the Makefile because every step of it needs
# a tty and the credential stores it fills are all in here.
COPY --chown=${USER_UID}:${USER_GID} login.sh /home/dev/login.sh

COPY --chown=${USER_UID}:${USER_GID} entrypoint.sh /home/dev/entrypoint.sh
# Sourced by it, from beside it: the functions that seed pi's agent dir, in a file of their
# own so dev/test_entrypoint_pi.sh can call them without running the entrypoint.
COPY --chown=${USER_UID}:${USER_GID} pi-seed.sh /home/dev/pi-seed.sh

CMD ["bash", "/home/dev/entrypoint.sh"]
